  "body": "Edit: _what follows below is the original post. To see the action item for a good first bug, go to https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/1914#issuecomment-254535812_\n\n---\n\nIn the HTML living spec, the `a` (anchor) element can have the `download` attribute to tell whether the link is intended for downloading: https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/semantics.html#attr-hyperlink-download\n\nWhen the link is valid and the server returns a HTTP 2xx or 3xx response, everything sounds fine.\n\nBut what about HTTP 4xx and HTTP 5xx error responses? Will the browser retrieve the error response body and try to save it in a file? I doubt this is what developer want the browser to do.\n\nIMO, conforming UA should download the linked resource if the server responds with an HTTP 2xx/3xx, but should consider the anchor link as a \"normal\" link otherwise (ie: if the HTTP response is not 2xx/3xx, then the link acts like `download` attribute was not set).\n",
